WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.970
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi, I'm Mike
Coleman, a developer advocate

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:03.810
at Google Cloud.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.230
And today I want to talk
about some key considerations

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.170
you should be aware of when
looking to migrate existing

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.495
applications from VMs to
containers using Migrate

00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:13.995
for Anthos.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.970
In a couple of our earlier
videos on Migrate for Anthos,

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.590
we covered how to
use the Discovery

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.720
tool to discover
what applications

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.280
are a good fit for migration.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.020
If you've not
checked those out you

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.150
may want to watch
them and come back.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.880
You can find the links
in the description below.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.770
For the remainder
of this video, I'm

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.950
going to assume that you've
already chosen an application,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.460
run the Discovery tool and found
that your application is well

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.320
suited for migration.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.030
While this is a
great place to be,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:50.800
there are some
additional questions

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.880
you'll want to answer before
proceeding with a migration.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.510
What should I do with any
databases I might be using,

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.630
how should I handle a VM with
multiple applications running

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:05.470
in it, how do my networking
requirements translate

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.750
to a Kubernetes deployment.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.540
Let's start by looking
at handling multiple apps

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.240
or services running
in a single VM.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.880
A common scenario we often
see is an application

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.640
where multiple components
are running on the same VM.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.490
For instance, perhaps
you have a web front end,

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.800
a middleware component,
and a database

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.560
all running together in a
monolithic virtual machine.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.490
Now ideally, and
with Kubernetes you

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.730
have a single process
running in each pod.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.950
However by default,
Migrate for Anthos

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.990
will not create a
separate container image

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.660
for each service running in VM.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.220
Since Migrate for
Anthos will try

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.000
to migrate each running service,
what you do in a situation

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.040
where you have multiple
application components

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.010
is to ensure that only
one is running when

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.750
you run the migration process.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.500
For instance, shut down the
middleware and the database,

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.010
run Migrate for Anthos, and
capture the web front end.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.419
Then shut down the web
front end and the database,

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:12.310
run Migrate for Anthos and
capture the middleware.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:15.160
With those two components
migrated into containers

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.050
you'll need to decide
what makes the most

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.090
sense for your database.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.040
With the database
tier you first need

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.290
to decide whether or not
you're dealing with a mission

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:24.970
critical component.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.445
In some cases, you might
be migrating or configuring

00:02:27.445 --> 00:02:30.100
a dev test deployment
of your application

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.870
or perhaps your
production application

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:33.820
is configured as
a monolith and you

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.110
don't want to break out
the separate components.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.680
Now you realize, of course,
that this configuration doesn't

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.720
offer you the ability to
easily scale or provide

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.790
fault tolerance, but
it works for what

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.330
you're trying to accomplish.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.760
In this case, you would
use Migrate for Anthos

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.430
to migrate the data to
a persistent volume,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.190
attach that volume to the
pod at the appropriate spot

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.780
in the file system.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.690
If it's possible to migrate
your database to Cloud SQL,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.130
that's a great choice.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.010
You can use Google Cloud's
database migration service

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.540
to migrate the data, then
configure your application

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.770
to use Cloud SQL or other
managed database service.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.420
From there, you can
start to benefit

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.330
from the advantages of running
your database on a managed

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:13.560
service offering.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.410
Now if Cloud SQL is not a
fit, then your next option

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.170
could be to run your database
natively on Kubernetes.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.570
You would build a cluster
and install and configure

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.730
the database software
using Kubernetes Stateful

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.070
Set and persistent volumes.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.760
Then use Migrate for Anthos
to export the data from the VM

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.950
to the new database.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.050
Finally, if running the
database on Kubernetes

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:37.260
is not a good fit, you could
migrate the database tier

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.500
into a Google Cloud
Compute Engine VM using

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.390
Migrate for Compute Engine.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.220
There's a great blog
post up on our site

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.690
providing some
additional details

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.300
on how to decide where
to run your database.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.720
We'll put the link in
the description below.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.490
Now that you have
individual images

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.080
for each of your
services, you'll

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.940
want to think about networking.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.910
In many cases different services
within an application are

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.820
accessed using a DNS
based resource name. ,

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.210
However when migrating to
Kubernetes you'll use a service

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:06.760
name.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.290
A good recommendation is to
choose short, yet descriptive

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:13.110
service names and ensure
your services are all

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:16.019
running in the correct
Kubernetes name space.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.230
One way to handle the move
from DNS names to service names

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:23.310
is to provide your service names
via a Kubernetes config map.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.410
So let's take a look at
using that config map

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.580
to alter the service discovery.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.780
So I have an application
that I migrated over,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.200
a service and an application.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.500
So if I do a get
services here, you

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.050
will see this ledger
monolith service.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.720
That came from a virtual
machine using Migrate for Anthos

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.270
but I have not changed
the DNS mapping

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.260
to Kubernetes service mapping.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.030
So it doesn't work.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:47.783
So I'm going to
go ahead and go in

00:04:47.783 --> 00:04:49.200
and we're going
to open the editor

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.690
and we're going to
edit the config map.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:55.183
And we're just going to
remove these DNS suffixes.

00:04:55.183 --> 00:04:56.850
And when we do that,
that will take that

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.030
from being a DNS based name
to the Kubernetes service

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.030
name, which is
ledgermonolith-service.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.680
So let me do this last one here.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.840
And then we'll save that out
and move back to our terminal

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.200
and let's clear the
screen and we're

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.520
going to go ahead and
apply the config map.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.560
And that's going to
update the services

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.068
on how they communicate.

00:05:19.068 --> 00:05:20.860
So if we clear that,
now what we need to do

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.690
is we need to restart
our pods and we'll

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:24.640
do that by deleting
them and then

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.540
they'll recreate automatically.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.960
So we'll delete them
and then let's come up

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.690
and let's watch those
pods get reloaded.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.390
And this will take a second,
so I've sped up the video

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.420
and then now we can
bounce out here and let's

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.670
take a look at our services
and make sure we've

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.000
got the right IP address.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.690
So we'll do a get service
and there's that IP, we'll

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:49.320
move up here into our browser.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.470
We'll do a refresh
and you can see

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:53.270
that it's actually working now.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:56.480
So that is using a
config map to move

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.780
from DNS based name resolution
with a virtual machine

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.780
to Kubernetes service
based name resolution using

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.230
the internal Kubernetes
service discovery mechanisms.

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:09.680
As an aside, when dealing with
sensitive configuration data,

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.780
you want to take advantage
of Kubernetes secrets.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.850
For instance, if you need
to provide a password

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:18.310
to connect to the database,
you can store that

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:22.150
in a Kubernetes secret
rather than in a config map.

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:24.100
Check the Kubernetes
documentation

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.280
for more information on how
to configure and use secrets.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.160
Now the next thing to
consider is controlling access

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.690
to your application.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:35.200
By default, the deployment
spec generated by Migrate

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.410
for Anthos contains a
suggested headless service

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.900
object of type cluster IP.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.410
This means no load balancing
and a single cluster internal IP

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.410
reachable only from
within that cluster.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.750
In the case of our
web front end service,

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.550
we want to change that
to the type load balancer

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.090
to allow external access.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.850
You should also consider using
some of Kubernetes network

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:00.160
policy features
to restrict access

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.290
to your various
application tiers.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.260
For instance, you might want
to use network policies that

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.120
allow only the web
front end to talk

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.520
to the middleware tier
and another policy that

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.795
allows only the middleware
tier to talk to the database.

00:07:12.795 --> 00:07:14.170
We've hit a few
of the highlights

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.610
with regards to
things you should

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:18.010
consider as you plan
your VM migration using

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:19.090
Migrate for Anthos.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:20.980
To learn more about
Migrate for Anthos,

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:23.960
be sure to check out the Migrate
for Anthos documentation.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.710
And this is one
video in a series,

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.590
so be on the lookout for other
Migrate for Anthos videos

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.570
and like and subscribe
to be notified

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.670
as we add additional content.

